{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJL7MJGQxMVwzNymzdpzkg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'DocRED FT+constrained results mirror SciERC: DR→0%, F1 preserved,\n2\t  composability universally confirmed. Paper gains composability dimension — OntoCoDec\n3\t  is orthogonal to model improvement, works out of the box with FT at zero cost. C8\n4\t  (low absolute F1) becomes non-issue since FT closes performance gap. Core claim:\n5\t  guaranteed type-validity as modular property persisting across training regimes.'\n6\timagined_experiments:\n7\t- name: Qwen DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\n8\t  plan_id: w5_qwen_docred_lora_constrained_eval\n9\t  description: 'Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Qwen3-8B\n10\t    LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Validates composability: does OntoCoDec eliminate residual\n11\t    DR from FT model without degrading the 40.86% R-F1 gained through fine-tuning?'\n12\t  expected_results:\n13\t    ET_pct: 0.0\n14\t    RV_pct: 0.0\n15\t    DR_pct: 0.0\n16\t    CF_pct: 0.38\n17\t    E_F1: 82.18\n18\t    E_P: 85.48\n19\t    E_R: 79.13\n20\t    R_F1: 40.92\n21\t    R_P: 36.47\n22\t    R_R: 46.6\n23\t    parse_fail_rate: 7.01\n24\t    total_samples: 998\n25\t    success_samples: 928\n26\t    fail_samples: 70\n27\t    total_entities_pred: 18095\n28\t    total_relations_pred: 15862\n29\t  result_rationale: 'FT already reduced DR from 18.46% (zero-shot) to 0.76% — the\n30\t    model learned ontology constraints during training, leaving only ~120 residual\n31\t    DR violations across 15870 relations. These residual violations are the \"long\n32\t    tail\" — relation types where the model occasionally assigns a borderline entity\n33\t    type. OntoCoDec''s semantic layer trivially catches these since (a) the FT model\n34\t    already produces valid relation names (RV=0.27%), so grammar layer has minimal\n35\t    work, and (b) with near-perfect vocabulary compliance, the semantic layer can\n36\t    reliably look up domain/range rules and mask the few remaining violations. DR→0%\n37\t    follows the exact pattern seen on SciERC FT (Qwen DR 1.95%→0%).\n38\t\n39\t    R-F1 preservation (Δ=+0.06pp) occurs because: (1) grammar eliminates the remaining\n40\t    0.27% RV, converting OOV relation predictions into valid ones — under all_set\n41\t    scoring this marginally helps precision; (2) semantic masking on a well-calibrated\n42\t    FT model redirects probability mass from the ~0.76% DR violations to nearby valid\n43\t    type assignments, which are often correct given FT''s strong calibration. Net\n44\t    effect is F1-neutral to marginally positive, within the ±0.2pp band.\n45\t\n46\t    CF remains very low (0.38%) because FT''s strong calibration means entity types\n47\t    are consistent across contexts — the semantic layer rarely needs to override the\n48\t    model''s type choice, so the EVR mechanism that caused 9.9% CF in zero-shot (where\n49\t    the model was uncertain about types) is largely absent. The few CF cases arise\n50\t    from genuinely ambiguous entities in DocRED''s large ontology.\n51\t\n52\t    Parse failure rate slightly improves (7.11%→7.01%) because grammar constraints\n53\t    ensure valid JSON structure, rescuing a few documents that would have failed in\n54\t    unconstrained mode.\n55\t\n56\t    '\n57\t  tables:\n58\t  - title: Qwen DocRED FT Composability Matrix\n59\t    rows:\n60\t    - condition: FT unconstrained\n61\t      DR: 0.76\n62\t      RV: 0.27\n63\t      ET: 0.0\n64\t      CF: 0.12\n65\t      R_F1: 40.86\n66\t      E_F1: 82.15\n67\t    - condition: FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n68\t      DR: 0.0\n69\t      RV: 0.0\n70\t      ET: 0.0\n71\t      CF: 0.38\n72\t      R_F1: 40.92\n73\t      E_F1: 82.18\n74\t    - condition: Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n75\t      DR: 10.21\n76\t      RV: 0.03\n77\t      ET: 0.0\n78\t      CF: 2.57\n79\t      R_F1: 8.23\n80\t      E_F1: 59.37\n81\t  figures_description:\n82\t  - 'Bar chart: DR reduction cascade — zero-shot (18.46%) → zero-shot+OntoCoDec (10.21%)\n83\t    → FT unconstrained (0.76%) → FT+OntoCoDec (0.0%). Shows FT and OntoCoDec as orthogonal\n84\t    DR eliminators.'\n85\t  - 'Scatter plot: per-relation DR before/after for FT+constrained. All 96 relations\n86\t    cluster at (x, 0) regardless of unconstrained residual rate, demonstrating universal\n87\t    elimination.'\n88\t  analysis: 'The Qwen DocRED FT+constrained result confirms the composability thesis\n89\t    at its strongest: OntoCoDec eliminates the last 0.76% DR residual that FT could\n90\t    not learn away, while preserving the 5× R-F1 improvement (8.23→40.86→40.92) that\n91\t    FT provides. The two methods are genuinely orthogonal — FT improves extraction\n92\t    quality, OntoCoDec guarantees type validity. Neither degrades the other.\n93\t\n94\t    The near-zero CF (0.38% vs 9.9% in zero-shot) is the key new finding: it proves\n95\t    that zero-shot CF was not an inherent limitation of OntoCoDec''s architecture,\n96\t    but a consequence of applying semantic constraints to an uncertain model. When\n97\t    the model is confident and well-calibrated (post-FT), the semantic layer''s overrides\n98\t    are rare and targeted, producing minimal consistency side-effects.\n99\t\n100\t    Parse failure rate stability (7.11%→7.01%) confirms that constrained decoding\n101\t    does not create new failure modes on FT models — a concern from zero-shot Llama\n102\t    where parse failures increased from 6.8% to 9.1%.\n103\t\n104\t    '\n105\t- name: Llama DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\n106\t  plan_id: w5_llama_docred_lora_constrained_eval\n107\t  description: 'Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Llama-3.1-8B\n108\t    LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Cross-model composability validation: does the composability\n109\t    pattern generalize beyond Qwen?'\n110\t  expected_results:\n111\t    ET_pct: 0.0\n112\t    RV_pct: 0.0\n113\t    DR_pct: 0.03\n114\t    CF_pct: 0.42\n115\t    E_F1: 83.89\n116\t    E_P: 84.9\n117\t    E_R: 82.91\n118\t    R_F1: 43.68\n119\t    R_P: 38.55\n120\t    R_R: 50.36\n121\t    parse_fail_rate: 2.3\n122\t    total_samples: 998\n123\t    total_entities_pred: 19098\n124\t    total_relations_pred: 16158\n125\t  result_rationale: 'Llama FT unconstrained already achieves DR=0.49% (even lower\n126\t    than Qwen''s 0.76%), with only ~79 residual DR violations across 16171 relations.\n127\t    The lower baseline reflects Llama''s stronger alignment with DocRED''s type system\n128\t    post-FT (also evidenced by its lower RV=0.15% vs Qwen 0.27%).\n129\t\n130\t    DR→0.03% (not exactly 0%, ~5 residual violations) because Llama''s FT residuals\n131\t    may include  a handful of edge cases where the semantic layer''s fallback mechanism\n132\t    triggers. However, this is within the [0.0, 0.1] best-tier band. The near-zero\n133\t    residual matches SciERC FT pattern exactly (Llama SciERC FT+constrained achieved\n134\t    DR=0.09%).\n135\t\n136\t    R-F1 Δ=-0.04pp (43.72→43.68) — negligible and within ±0.2pp band. The tiny negative\n137\t    delta occurs because Llama''s RV baseline is already very low (0.15%), so grammar\n138\t    provides minimal FP-elimination benefit, while the semantic layer''s rare interventions\n139\t    on ~79 triples occasionally redirect to a type that doesn''t match gold. This\n140\t    is the same pattern as Llama SciERC FT+constrained (R-F1 45.45→45.47, Δ=+0.02pp)\n141\t    — essentially noise around zero.\n142\t\n143\t    CF=0.42% is slightly higher than Qwen (0.38%) but still well below the 0.5% threshold.\n144\t    Llama showed higher CF sensitivity in zero-shot (12.02% on DocRED vs Qwen''s 2.57%),\n145\t    but FT''s calibration effect suppresses this just as effectively. The small CF\n146\t    residual reflects Llama''s slightly higher tendency toward entity-type drift in\n147\t    complex documents.\n148\t\n149\t    Parse failure rate holds at 2.3% (unchanged from unconstrained) because Llama''s\n150\t    FT model already produces well-structured JSON, and grammar constraints neither\n151\t    help nor hurt. This contrasts with zero-shot Llama where constrained decoding\n152\t    increased parse failures (6.8%→9.1%) — FT resolves the underlying generation instability\n153\t    that caused those failures.\n154\t\n155\t    '\n156\t  tables:\n157\t  - title: Llama DocRED FT Composability Matrix\n158\t    rows:\n159\t    - condition: FT unconstrained\n160\t      DR: 0.49\n161\t      RV: 0.15\n162\t      ET: 0.0\n163\t      CF: 0.0\n164\t      R_F1: 43.72\n165\t      E_F1: 83.93\n166\t    - condition: FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n167\t      DR: 0.03\n168\t      RV: 0.0\n169\t      ET: 0.0\n170\t      CF: 0.42\n171\t      R_F1: 43.68\n172\t      E_F1: 83.89\n173\t    - condition: Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n174\t      DR: 2.55\n175\t      RV: 0.0\n176\t      ET: 0.0\n177\t      CF: 12.02\n178\t      R_F1: 6.27\n179\t      E_F1: 55.01\n180\t  - title: Cross-Model FT+Constrained Summary (DocRED)\n181\t    rows:\n182\t    - model: Qwen3-8B\n183\t      DR_unconstrained: 0.76\n184\t      DR_constrained: 0.0\n185\t      DR_reduction: 100%\n186\t      R_F1_delta_pp: '+0.06'\n187\t      CF_constrained: 0.38\n188\t      parse_fail_delta: '-0.10'\n189\t    - model: Llama-3.1-8B\n190\t      DR_unconstrained: 0.49\n191\t      DR_constrained: 0.03\n192\t      DR_reduction: 93.9%\n193\t      R_F1_delta_pp: '-0.04'\n194\t      CF_constrained: 0.42\n195\t      parse_fail_delta: '0.00'\n196\t  figures_description:\n197\t  - '2×2 composability heatmap: rows = {zero-shot, FT}, columns = {unconstrained,\n198\t    +OntoCoDec}. Cells show DR%. Pattern: FT reduces DR by ~97%, OntoCoDec eliminates\n199\t    remainder. Both models show same pattern.'\n200\t  - 'Line plot: CF rate across settings. Zero-shot+OntoCoDec shows high CF (Qwen 2.57%,\n201\t    Llama 12.02%); FT+OntoCoDec collapses CF to <0.5% for both models. Demonstrates\n202\t    FT calibration resolves CF limitation.'\n203\t  - 'Paired bar chart: R-F1 before/after OntoCoDec for FT models. Both models show\n204\t    bars of indistinguishable height (Δ < 0.1pp), visually confirming zero-cost composability.'\n205\t  analysis: 'Llama''s results complete the 2×2×2 matrix (2 models × 2 datasets × 2\n206\t    constraint settings for FT). The composability claim is now supported by 4 independent\n207\t    datapoints, all showing the same pattern: DR→≈0%, R-F1 within ±0.2pp, CF<0.5%.\n208\t\n209\t    The most striking finding is the CF collapse: Llama went from 12.02% CF (zero-shot+constrained)\n210\t    to 0.42% (FT+constrained) — a 96.5% reduction. This transforms the CF limitation\n211\t    story entirely. CF was never an architectural defect of OntoCoDec; it was a symptom\n212\t    of applying type constraints to a model that hadn''t learned the type system.\n213\t    Once the model is calibrated via FT, OntoCoDec''s per-relation enforcement produces\n214\t    consistent types because the model''s own type priors are already consistent —\n215\t    the semantic layer merely enforces a boundary the model rarely approaches.\n216\t\n217\t    Paper narrative upgrade: \"OntoCoDec is a modular compliance layer. Applied to\n218\t    a zero-shot model, it eliminates 90-99% of DR violations with elevated CF as a\n219\t    known trade-off. Applied to a fine-tuned model, it eliminates 94-100% of residual\n220\t    DR at zero cost — no F1 loss, no CF elevation, no parse failure increase. The\n221\t    two interventions are orthogonal and compose freely.\"\n222\t\n223\t    This directly addresses C8 (low absolute F1): FT achieves 40-44% R-F1 (competitive\n224\t    with supervised methods given the zero-shot-to-few-shot gap), and OntoCoDec adds\n225\t    guaranteed compliance on top. The practical deployment story is complete: train\n226\t    your model, add OntoCoDec, get guaranteed type-valid output at zero marginal cost.'\n227\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c16ed721-7280-4150-92ce-92060067d621","uuid":"72f813b6-f758-43dd-862d-d70b4f57b6e4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:42:13.266Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_6/projects/schema_semantic_constrained_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T021434/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'DocRED FT+constrained results mirror SciERC: DR→0%, F1 preserved,\n  composability universally confirmed. Paper gains composability dimension — OntoCoDec\n  is orthogonal to model improvement, works out of the box with FT at zero cost. C8\n  (low absolute F1) becomes non-issue since FT closes performance gap. Core claim:\n  guaranteed type-validity as modular property persisting across training regimes.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Qwen DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\n  plan_id: w5_qwen_docred_lora_constrained_eval\n  description: 'Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Qwen3-8B\n    LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Validates composability: does OntoCoDec eliminate residual\n    DR from FT model without degrading the 40.86% R-F1 gained through fine-tuning?'\n  expected_results:\n    ET_pct: 0.0\n    RV_pct: 0.0\n    DR_pct: 0.0\n    CF_pct: 0.38\n    E_F1: 82.18\n    E_P: 85.48\n    E_R: 79.13\n    R_F1: 40.92\n    R_P: 36.47\n    R_R: 46.6\n    parse_fail_rate: 7.01\n    total_samples: 998\n    success_samples: 928\n    fail_samples: 70\n    total_entities_pred: 18095\n    total_relations_pred: 15862\n  result_rationale: 'FT already reduced DR from 18.46% (zero-shot) to 0.76% — the\n    model learned ontology constraints during training, leaving only ~120 residual\n    DR violations across 15870 relations. These residual violations are the \"long\n    tail\" — relation types where the model occasionally assigns a borderline entity\n    type. OntoCoDec''s semantic layer trivially catches these since (a) the FT model\n    already produces valid relation names (RV=0.27%), so grammar layer has minimal\n    work, and (b) with near-perfect vocabulary compliance, the semantic layer can\n    reliably look up domain/range rules and mask the few remaining violations. DR→0%\n    follows the exact pattern seen on SciERC FT (Qwen DR 1.95%→0%).\n\n    R-F1 preservation (Δ=+0.06pp) occurs because: (1) grammar eliminates the remaining\n    0.27% RV, converting OOV relation predictions into valid ones — under all_set\n    scoring this marginally helps precision; (2) semantic masking on a well-calibrated\n    FT model redirects probability mass from the ~0.76% DR violations to nearby valid\n    type assignments, which are often correct given FT''s strong calibration. Net\n    effect is F1-neutral to marginally positive, within the ±0.2pp band.\n\n    CF remains very low (0.38%) because FT''s strong calibration means entity types\n    are consistent across contexts — the semantic layer rarely needs to override the\n    model''s type choice, so the EVR mechanism that caused 9.9% CF in zero-shot (where\n    the model was uncertain about types) is largely absent. The few CF cases arise\n    from genuinely ambiguous entities in DocRED''s large ontology.\n\n    Parse failure rate slightly improves (7.11%→7.01%) because grammar constraints\n    ensure valid JSON structure, rescuing a few documents that would have failed in\n    unconstrained mode.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Qwen DocRED FT Composability Matrix\n    rows:\n    - condition: FT unconstrained\n      DR: 0.76\n      RV: 0.27\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 0.12\n      R_F1: 40.86\n      E_F1: 82.15\n    - condition: FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n      DR: 0.0\n      RV: 0.0\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 0.38\n      R_F1: 40.92\n      E_F1: 82.18\n    - condition: Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n      DR: 10.21\n      RV: 0.03\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 2.57\n      R_F1: 8.23\n      E_F1: 59.37\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: DR reduction cascade — zero-shot (18.46%) → zero-shot+OntoCoDec (10.21%)\n    → FT unconstrained (0.76%) → FT+OntoCoDec (0.0%). Shows FT and OntoCoDec as orthogonal\n    DR eliminators.'\n  - 'Scatter plot: per-relation DR before/after for FT+constrained. All 96 relations\n    cluster at (x, 0) regardless of unconstrained residual rate, demonstrating universal\n    elimination.'\n  analysis: 'The Qwen DocRED FT+constrained result confirms the composability thesis\n    at its strongest: OntoCoDec eliminates the last 0.76% DR residual that FT could\n    not learn away, while preserving the 5× R-F1 improvement (8.23→40.86→40.92) that\n    FT provides. The two methods are genuinely orthogonal — FT improves extraction\n    quality, OntoCoDec guarantees type validity. Neither degrades the other.\n\n    The near-zero CF (0.38% vs 9.9% in zero-shot) is the key new finding: it proves\n    that zero-shot CF was not an inherent limitation of OntoCoDec''s architecture,\n    but a consequence of applying semantic constraints to an uncertain model. When\n    the model is confident and well-calibrated (post-FT), the semantic layer''s overrides\n    are rare and targeted, producing minimal consistency side-effects.\n\n    Parse failure rate stability (7.11%→7.01%) confirms that constrained decoding\n    does not create new failure modes on FT models — a concern from zero-shot Llama\n    where parse failures increased from 6.8% to 9.1%.\n\n    '\n- name: Llama DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\n  plan_id: w5_llama_docred_lora_constrained_eval\n  description: 'Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Llama-3.1-8B\n    LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Cross-model composability validation: does the composability\n    pattern generalize beyond Qwen?'\n  expected_results:\n    ET_pct: 0.0\n    RV_pct: 0.0\n    DR_pct: 0.03\n    CF_pct: 0.42\n    E_F1: 83.89\n    E_P: 84.9\n    E_R: 82.91\n    R_F1: 43.68\n    R_P: 38.55\n    R_R: 50.36\n    parse_fail_rate: 2.3\n    total_samples: 998\n    total_entities_pred: 19098\n    total_relations_pred: 16158\n  result_rationale: 'Llama FT unconstrained already achieves DR=0.49% (even lower\n    than Qwen''s 0.76%), with only ~79 residual DR violations across 16171 relations.\n    The lower baseline reflects Llama''s stronger alignment with DocRED''s type system\n    post-FT (also evidenced by its lower RV=0.15% vs Qwen 0.27%).\n\n    DR→0.03% (not exactly 0%, ~5 residual violations) because Llama''s FT residuals\n    may include  a handful of edge cases where the semantic layer''s fallback mechanism\n    triggers. However, this is within the [0.0, 0.1] best-tier band. The near-zero\n    residual matches SciERC FT pattern exactly (Llama SciERC FT+constrained achieved\n    DR=0.09%).\n\n    R-F1 Δ=-0.04pp (43.72→43.68) — negligible and within ±0.2pp band. The tiny negative\n    delta occurs because Llama''s RV baseline is already very low (0.15%), so grammar\n    provides minimal FP-elimination benefit, while the semantic layer''s rare interventions\n    on ~79 triples occasionally redirect to a type that doesn''t match gold. This\n    is the same pattern as Llama SciERC FT+constrained (R-F1 45.45→45.47, Δ=+0.02pp)\n    — essentially noise around zero.\n\n    CF=0.42% is slightly higher than Qwen (0.38%) but still well below the 0.5% threshold.\n    Llama showed higher CF sensitivity in zero-shot (12.02% on DocRED vs Qwen''s 2.57%),\n    but FT''s calibration effect suppresses this just as effectively. The small CF\n    residual reflects Llama''s slightly higher tendency toward entity-type drift in\n    complex documents.\n\n    Parse failure rate holds at 2.3% (unchanged from unconstrained) because Llama''s\n    FT model already produces well-structured JSON, and grammar constraints neither\n    help nor hurt. This contrasts with zero-shot Llama where constrained decoding\n    increased parse failures (6.8%→9.1%) — FT resolves the underlying generation instability\n    that caused those failures.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Llama DocRED FT Composability Matrix\n    rows:\n    - condition: FT unconstrained\n      DR: 0.49\n      RV: 0.15\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 0.0\n      R_F1: 43.72\n      E_F1: 83.93\n    - condition: FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n      DR: 0.03\n      RV: 0.0\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 0.42\n      R_F1: 43.68\n      E_F1: 83.89\n    - condition: Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\n      DR: 2.55\n      RV: 0.0\n      ET: 0.0\n      CF: 12.02\n      R_F1: 6.27\n      E_F1: 55.01\n  - title: Cross-Model FT+Constrained Summary (DocRED)\n    rows:\n    - model: Qwen3-8B\n      DR_unconstrained: 0.76\n      DR_constrained: 0.0\n      DR_reduction: 100%\n      R_F1_delta_pp: '+0.06'\n      CF_constrained: 0.38\n      parse_fail_delta: '-0.10'\n    - model: Llama-3.1-8B\n      DR_unconstrained: 0.49\n      DR_constrained: 0.03\n      DR_reduction: 93.9%\n      R_F1_delta_pp: '-0.04'\n      CF_constrained: 0.42\n      parse_fail_delta: '0.00'\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 composability heatmap: rows = {zero-shot, FT}, columns = {unconstrained,\n    +OntoCoDec}. Cells show DR%. Pattern: FT reduces DR by ~97%, OntoCoDec eliminates\n    remainder. Both models show same pattern.'\n  - 'Line plot: CF rate across settings. Zero-shot+OntoCoDec shows high CF (Qwen 2.57%,\n    Llama 12.02%); FT+OntoCoDec collapses CF to <0.5% for both models. Demonstrates\n    FT calibration resolves CF limitation.'\n  - 'Paired bar chart: R-F1 before/after OntoCoDec for FT models. Both models show\n    bars of indistinguishable height (Δ < 0.1pp), visually confirming zero-cost composability.'\n  analysis: 'Llama''s results complete the 2×2×2 matrix (2 models × 2 datasets × 2\n    constraint settings for FT). The composability claim is now supported by 4 independent\n    datapoints, all showing the same pattern: DR→≈0%, R-F1 within ±0.2pp, CF<0.5%.\n\n    The most striking finding is the CF collapse: Llama went from 12.02% CF (zero-shot+constrained)\n    to 0.42% (FT+constrained) — a 96.5% reduction. This transforms the CF limitation\n    story entirely. CF was never an architectural defect of OntoCoDec; it was a symptom\n    of applying type constraints to a model that hadn''t learned the type system.\n    Once the model is calibrated via FT, OntoCoDec''s per-relation enforcement produces\n    consistent types because the model''s own type priors are already consistent —\n    the semantic layer merely enforces a boundary the model rarely approaches.\n\n    Paper narrative upgrade: \"OntoCoDec is a modular compliance layer. Applied to\n    a zero-shot model, it eliminates 90-99% of DR violations with elevated CF as a\n    known trade-off. Applied to a fine-tuned model, it eliminates 94-100% of residual\n    DR at zero cost — no F1 loss, no CF elevation, no parse failure increase. The\n    two interventions are orthogonal and compose freely.\"\n\n    This directly addresses C8 (low absolute F1): FT achieves 40-44% R-F1 (competitive\n    with supervised methods given the zero-shot-to-few-shot gap), and OntoCoDec adds\n    guaranteed compliance on top. The practical deployment story is complete: train\n    your model, add OntoCoDec, get guaranteed type-valid output at zero marginal cost.'\n","numLines":227,"startLine":1,"totalLines":227}}}
